A(app(C, "Chiloé", "DO", "Chiloé became a Chilean DO subregion in 2025: Decree 27 of November 2024, published on 14 May 2025, added the whole archipelago of Chiloé province to the Austral region. The decree's own reasoning is striking: climate change has widened the latitude where vines can grow, and the islands' insular conditions now produce distinctive quality wine. Plantings near 42°S are minuscule, mainly cool-climate whites and Pinot Noir, and the DO is one of the southernmost legally defined wine areas anywhere, though Argentina's Chubut IGs reach further south.",
      match=["Chiloé", "Chiloe"], region=R, created="2025 (Decreto 27 of 2024)", styles=["white", "red", "sparkling"], dominant_style="white",
      grapes="Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling", lat=-42.48, lon=-73.77, coord_quality="approx",
      nerd_note="The same 2025 decree created a DO for Rapa Nui (Easter Island), 3,700 km out in the Pacific."))
A(app(C, "Rapa Nui - Isla de Pascua", "DO", "Almost certainly the most remote wine appellation in the world: Easter Island, about 3,700 km off the Chilean coast in the South Pacific, became a Chilean DO area through Decree 27 (signed November 2024, published May 2025). It covers the island's entire commune and sits outside every viticultural region, listed on its own as numeral 7 of article 1. The government's stated aim was to give the island's handful of subtropical vineyards the same labelling rights as mainland wine; volumes are tiny and the subtropical, humid climate makes any wine here an experiment.",
      match=["Rapa Nui - Isla de Pascua", "Rapa Nui", "Isla de Pascua", "Easter Island"], region=None, created="2025 (Decreto 27 of 2024)",
      styles=["white", "red"], lat=-27.1500, lon=-109.4100, coord_quality="approx",
      nerd_note="Rapa Nui is the only Chilean DO not attached to any viticultural region or subregion."))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ SPECIAL
A(app(C, "Secano Interior", "DO", "Secano Interior ('dry interior') is a special denomination of origin for wines made only from País and Cinsault grown on unirrigated land in the coastal hills and interior of Maule, Ñuble and Biobío. It rescues a category the 1994 law had effectively excluded: País was not even on the list of varieties allowed for DO wine. The decree names the qualifying areas (Rauco, Romeral, Molina, Sagrada Familia, Talca, Pencahue, San Clemente, San Rafael, San Javier, Villa Alegre, Parral, Linares, Cauquenes, Chillán, Quillón, Portezuelo, Coelemu and Yumbel) plus further communes; in July 2026 Decree 4 added Hualqui, Nacimiento, Los Ángeles, Laja, San Rosendo, Santa Juana and Tomé in Biobío.\n\nThe label can read 'Secano Interior' followed by the subregion, zone or area. SAG recorded 5.4 million litres under this DO in 2026, small but symbolic: it gives legal standing to the dry-farmed, own-rooted, often centenarian bush vines that are now Chile's hottest wine story.",
      match=["Secano Interior", "DO Secano Interior", "Denominación de Origen Especial Secano Interior"], region="Valle Central",
      created="amendment of Decree 464 (art. 3 bis); extended 2026", styles=["red", "rose"], dominant_style="red", grapes="País (Mission, Criolla), Cinsault",
      lat=-36.0, lon=-72.3, coord_quality="approx",
      nerd_note="The decree formally recognises Mission and Criolla as accepted synonyms of País, linking Chile's grape to California's Mission and Argentina's Criolla Chica (all Listán Prieto)."))
